  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <div class="faq">
    <h3>Does responding to Google reviews help SEO?</h3>
    <p>It can help indirectly. Google lists responding to reviews in its own tips for improving local ranking, noting that it shows you value your customers and their feedback. Google does not say replies are a direct ranking factor, so treat them as part of an actively managed profile rather than a ranking trick.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>How quickly should I respond to Google reviews?</h3>
    <p>Within a few days is a sensible habit. Speed mainly matters to the customers reading your profile; Google has not said reply speed affects ranking.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>Should I include keywords in my Google review responses?</h3>
    <p>Yes, but naturally. Include your business type and location organically — such as "our restaurant in London" or "our dental clinic." Keyword stuffing looks robotic and hurts credibility.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq">
    <h3>Does the length of my review response matter for SEO?</h3>
    <p>Not for ranking, as far as Google has said. For readers, 2 to 5 sentences is usually right: long enough to be specific, short enough to be read.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq" style="border:none">
    <h3>Will responding to reviews get me more customers?</h3>
    <p>Replies are public, so they work as reassurance for the next customer: they show someone reads feedback and fixes problems. That is the main benefit, whatever the ranking effect.</p>
  </div>

